# 📘 Fakebook - OSINT

A fictional, Facebook-style social media clone built for **open-source intelligence (OSINT) training**. Fakebook gives learners realistic fake profiles — complete with posts, photos, friends, life events, and relationship details — to practice OSINT reconnaissance techniques in a safe, self-contained environment with zero real-world privacy impact.

**🔗 Live demo:** [fakebook-osint.netlify.app](https://fakebook-osint.netlify.app/)

## About

Fakebook was built by [Cyber Pathways](https://cyberpathways.com.au/) as a training ground for students and cybersecurity learners to practice open-source intelligence gathering — the skill of piecing together publicly available information into a fuller picture of a person or organisation.

Every profile, name, photo, post, comment, and "friend" on the site is entirely fictional. The layout parodies a well-known social platform purely so the OSINT exercises feel realistic, but **Fakebook has no affiliation with Meta Platforms, Inc. or any real company or person.**

> **Note:** This project is for educational and training purposes only. It is a static front-end mock-up — there are no real user accounts, no real data collection, and no backend services processing personal information.

## Features

- 👤 Multiple fictional profile pages with detailed "About" info (job history, education, relationship status, hometown, etc.)
- 📝 Realistic timeline of posts, photos, life events, and check-ins to practice piecing together a timeline
- 🖼️ Photo and video galleries per profile
- 👥 Friends lists linking between fictional profiles, useful for practicing relationship-mapping
- 💬 Comment threads and reactions on posts for context clues
- 📱 Responsive layout resembling a familiar social media UI

## How It's Used

1. Browse a fictional profile as you would a real social media page.
2. Practice OSINT skills such as building a timeline, mapping relationships, and identifying patterns (locations, workplaces, routines) from publicly "posted" information.
3. Use findings to complete challenge questions or scenarios set by an instructor or cyber challenge organiser.

## Tech Stack

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **HTML5** | Page and profile structure |
